n punitive intervention as proof of its predictive accuracy. Federal procurement guidelines currently lack any enforcement mechanism to audit these feedback loops once deployed in clinical or judicial settings. Hospital administrators and judges accept vendor output as scientific fact because it shields them from personal liability when a patient dies or a defendant languishes in jail.\n\nMandating public transparency and bias audits will not fix this failure as long as private vendors control the source code and training weights. Requiring algorithmic accountability demands independent, mandatory pre-deployment testing by civil rights technical experts with subpoena power over proprietary datasets. If the FDA can demand clinical trials for a pharmaceutical drug before market entry, federal procurement rules must demand equivalent validation for software determining who receives ICU beds or prison sentences. Until federal regulators tie public healthcare and justice funding to open-source algorithmic auditing, deployment remains an unregulated corporate experiment on marginalized populations.",
